  args: [
    %ShotDs.Data.Type{goal: :i, args: []},
    %ShotDs.Data.Type{goal: :o, args: [%ShotDs.Data.Type{goal: :i, args: []}]}
  ]
}
```

---

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Signature).__
A _signature_ $\Sigma$ is a finite set of declarations $c : \sigma$ with $\sigma \in \mathfrak{T}$, in which every constant symbol $c$ is declared at most once.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Free Variable, Parameter).__
Let $\mathcal{V} = \biguplus_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{V}_\tau$ and $\mathcal{P} = \biguplus_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{P}_\tau$ be families of countably infinite, pairwise disjoint sets with $\mathcal{V} \cap \mathcal{P} = \emptyset$. Elements of $\mathcal{V}$ are _free variables_ ($X, Y$); elements of $\mathcal{P}$ are _parameters_ ($p, q$).

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Term).__
Given a signature $\Sigma$, the set of _terms_ $\Lambda(\Sigma)$ is defined by

$$
\Lambda(\Sigma) \ni s, t \coloneqq c\langle \bar{\rho}_n \rangle \mid X \mid p \mid i \mid \lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t \mid s(\bar{t}_n)
$$

where $c \in \Sigma$ is a constant symbol, $\bar{\rho}_n, \bar{\tau}_n \in \mathcal{T}$ with $n \geq 0$, $X \in \mathcal{V}$, $p \in \mathcal{P}$, and $i \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$ is a de Bruijn index.

We identify $\lambda ().\, t$ with $t$ and $s()$ with $s$, and further identify $\lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, \lambda \bar{\upsilon}_m.\, t$ with $\lambda \bar{\tau}_n, \bar{\upsilon}_m.\, t$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

> Constants, variables and parameters are all implemented as `Declaration`. Note that we merge the definitions of constants and parameters and give parameters a unique `reference` as name. We also make the name of bound variables explicit and refer to them by their local index.

```elixir
defmodule Declaration_ do
  @enforce_keys [:kind, :name, :type]
  defstruct [:kind, :name, :type]

  @type t :: free_var_t() | bound_var_t() | const_t()

  @type free_var_t :: %__MODULE__{
          kind: :fv,
          name: var_name_t(),
          type: Type_.t()
        }
  @type bound_var_t :: %__MODULE__{
          kind: :bv,
          name: pos_integer(),
          type: Type_.t()
        }
  @type const_t :: %__MODULE__{
          kind: :co,
          name: const_name_t(),
          type: Type_.t()
        }

  @type var_name_t :: String.t() | reference() | pos_integer()
  @type const_name_t :: String.t() | reference()
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Declaration_, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 15, ...>>, ...}
```

> Terms as the main data structure require more thought in their representation to optimize both space and time efficiency. We opt for _hash-consed directed acyclic graphs_ (DAGs) and rely on the Erlang term storage (ETS), a heavily optimized global hash table, for efficient and concurrency-safe lookup. Our term representation features 5 data fields and 4 metadata fields, optimized for caching and minimizing recursive checks:

```elixir
defmodule Term_ do
  @enforce_keys [:id, :head, :type]
  defstruct [
    # Data fields
    :id,
    :head,
    :type,
    bvars: [],
    args: [],
    # Metadata fields
    fvars: MapSet.new(),
    consts: MapSet.new(),
    tvars: MapSet.new(),
    max_num: 0
  ]

  @type global_term_id :: pos_integer()
  @type local_term_id :: neg_integer()
  @type dummy_term_id :: 0

  @type term_id :: global_term_id() | local_term_id() | dummy_term_id()

  @type t :: %__MODULE__{
          id: term_id(),
          bvars: [Declaration_.t()],
          head: Declaration_.t(),
          args: [term_id()],
          type: Type_.t(),
          fvars: MapSet.t(Declaration_.t()),
          consts: MapSet.t(Declaration_.const_t()),
          tvars: MapSet.t(Type_.variable_id()),
          max_num: non_neg_integer()
        }
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Term_, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 19, ...>>, ...}
```

> Terms are constructed globally, via the `ShotDs.Stt.TermFactory` module in a single ETS table (`:term_pool`). You may have noticed that term IDs may be _global_ or _local_. Global term IDs are stored in the `:term_pool` while local IDs only persist in a process-local ETS table, the _scratchpad_. Garbage collection is implemented so that result terms (and transitively, their argument terms) are committed to the `:term_pool` while the scratchpad with all its entries is deleted. Garbage collection can be enabled by wrapping the code block in `with_scratchpad(fn -> ... end)`.
> 
> Treating terms as global DAGs lets us write efficient canonical higher-order functions such as `map_term` or `fold_term` using term IDs as keys for caching:

```elixir
defmodule TermTraversal_ do
  @spec map_term!(
    term_id :: Term_.term_id(),
    env :: a,
    update_env :: (Term_.t(), a -> a),
    transform :: (Term_.t(), [Term_.term_id()], a, map() -> {Term_.term_id(), map()}),
    short_circuit :: (Term_.t(), a -> boolean()),
    cache :: map()
  ) :: {Term_.term_id(), map()}
    when a: var
  def map_term!(
      term_id,
      env,
      update_env,
      transform,
      short_circuit \\ fn _, _ -> false end,
      cache \\ %{}
    ) do
    case Map.fetch(cache, {term_id, env}) do
      {:ok, cached_id} -> {cached_id, cache}
      :error ->
        term = StubTF.get_term!(term_id)
        if short_circuit.(term, env) do
          {term_id, Map.put(cache, {term_id, env}, term_id)}
        else
          new_env = update_env.(term, env)
          arg_map_fn = &map_term!(&1, new_env, update_env, transform, short_circuit, &2)
          {new_args, cache} = Enum.map_reduce(term.args, cache, arg_map_fn)
          {new_id, cache} = transform.(term, new_args, new_env, cache)
          {new_id, Map.put(cache, {term_id, env}, new_id)}
        end
    end
  end
end

defmodule StubTF do
  @type_o %Type_{goal: :o}
  @dummy_head %Declaration_{kind: :co, name: "h", type: @type_o}
  def get_term!(term_id), do: %Term_{id: term_id, head: @dummy_head, type: @type_o}
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, StubTF, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 11, ...>>, ...}
```

__Definition (Context).__
A _context_ $\Gamma$ is a finite sequence of types $\bar{\tau}_n$, listed from outermost to innermost binding. Lookup $\Gamma(i)$ is defined by

$$
(\Gamma, \tau)(1) = \tau \qquad\qquad (\Gamma, \tau)(i+1) = \Gamma(i)
$$

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Typing).__
The typing judgement $\Gamma \vdash_\Sigma t : \tau$ is defined by the following rules; we drop the subscript $\Sigma$ when it is fixed.

$$
\frac{c : [\bar{\alpha}_n].\, \tau \in \Sigma \qquad \bar{\rho}_n \in \mathcal{T}}{\Gamma \vdash c\langle \bar{\rho}_n \rangle : \tau[\bar{\alpha}_n \mapsto \bar{\rho}_n]}
\qquad
\frac{X \in \mathcal{V}_\tau}{\Gamma \vdash X : \tau}
\qquad
\frac{p \in \mathcal{P}_\tau}{\Gamma \vdash p : \tau}
\qquad
\frac{\Gamma(i) = \tau}{\Gamma \vdash i : \tau}
$$

$$
\frac{\Gamma, \bar{\tau}_n \vdash t : \bar{\upsilon}_m \!\to\! \varsigma}{\Gamma \vdash \lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t : \bar{\tau}_n, \bar{\upsilon}_m \!\to\! \varsigma}
\qquad\qquad
\frac{\Gamma \vdash s : \bar{\tau}_n \!\to\! \varsigma \qquad \Gamma \vdash \bar{t}_n : \bar{\tau}_n}{\Gamma \vdash s(\bar{t}_n) : \varsigma}
$$

A term $t$ is _well-typed in $\Gamma$_ if $\Gamma \vdash t : \tau$ holds for some $\tau$. From here on, we only consider well-typed terms.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Proposition (Unique Typing).__
If $\Gamma \vdash t : \tau$ and $\Gamma \vdash t : \tau'$, then $\tau = \tau'$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Shifting).__
For $d \geq 0$ and cutoff $k \geq 0$, the _shift_ $t \uparrow^{d}_{k}$ is defined by

$$
i \uparrow^{d}_{k} = \begin{cases} i + d & i > k \\ i & i \leq k \end{cases}
\qquad
(\lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t) \uparrow^{d}_{k} = \lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t \uparrow^{d}_{k+n}
\qquad
(s(\bar{t}_n)) \uparrow^{d}_{k} = (s \uparrow^{d}_{k})(\bar{t}_n \uparrow^{d}_{k})
$$

together with $c\langle \bar{\rho}_n \rangle \uparrow^{d}_{k} = c\langle \bar{\rho}_n \rangle$, $X \uparrow^{d}_{k} = X$ and $p \uparrow^{d}_{k} = p$. We write $t \uparrow^{d}$ for $t \uparrow^{d}_{0}$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Substitution).__
The simultaneous substitution of a block $\bar{u}_n$ for the innermost $n$ indices, $t[\bar{u}_n]$, is $t[\bar{u}_n]_0$ where

$$
i[\bar{u}_n]_k = \begin{cases} u_{n+1-(i-k)} \uparrow^{k} & k < i \leq k+n \\ i - n & i > k+n \\ i & i \leq k \end{cases}
\qquad\qquad
(\lambda \bar{\tau}_m.\, t)[\bar{u}_n]_k = \lambda \bar{\tau}_m.\, t[\bar{u}_n]_{k+m}
$$

together with $(s(\bar{t}_m))[\bar{u}_n]_k = (s[\bar{u}_n]_k)(\bar{t}_m[\bar{u}_n]_k)$, and with constants, free variables and parameters left unchanged.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Free-Variable Substitution).__
A _free-variable substitution_ $\theta$ is a map from a finite subset of $\mathcal{V}$ to $\Lambda(\Sigma)$ such that $\theta(X)$ is ground of type $\tau$ for every $X \in \mathcal{V}_\tau \cap \operatorname{dom}(\theta)$. Its application $t\theta$ is $t\theta_0$ where

$$
X\theta_k = \begin{cases} \theta(X) \uparrow^{k} & X \in \operatorname{dom}(\theta) \\ X & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
\qquad\qquad
(\lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t)\theta_k = \lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t\theta_{k+n}
$$

together with $(s(\bar{t}_n))\theta_k = (s\theta_k)(\bar{t}_n\theta_k)$, and with constants, parameters and indices left unchanged.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

> Shifting and substitution are efficiently implemented on top of the `map_term` combinator. Both make use of the `env` field to store the current index depth and use a term's `max_num` and `fvars` fields to skip unnecessary recursive calls (as `short_circuit`).

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition ($\beta$-Reduction).__ 
$(\lambda \bar{\tau}_n.\, t)(\bar{u}_n, \bar{v}_m) \;\longrightarrow_\beta\; t[\bar{u}_n](\bar{v}_m)$

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Head).__
A _head_ is a term of the form $c\langle \bar{\rho}_n \rangle$, $X$, $p$ or $i$, ranged over by $h$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Long Normal Form).__
A well-typed term $t$ with $\Gamma \vdash t : \tau$ is in _$\eta$-long $\beta$-normal form_ if $t = \lambda \bar{\upsilon}_n.\, h(\bar{u}_m)$ where $n = \operatorname{ar}(\tau)$ and each $u_j$ is in $\eta$-long $\beta$-normal form. In the following, we only consider terms that are in $\eta$-long $\beta$-normal form and assume that they are always $\beta$-reduced and $\eta$-expanded after each transformation.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

> The internals of the `TermFactory` and `Semantics` API ensure terms to always be well-typed and in $\eta$-long $\beta$-normal form.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Parameter Term).__
Let $p \in \mathcal{P}_{\bar{\tau}_n, \bar{\upsilon}_m \to \varsigma}$ and let $\bar{t}_n$ be terms with $\Gamma \vdash \bar{t}_n : \bar{\tau}_n$. The _parameter term_ of $p$ over $\bar{t}_n$ is

$$
p\{\bar{t}_n\} \;=\; \lambda \bar{\upsilon}_m.\, p(\bar{t}_n \uparrow^{m}, m, \dots, 1)
$$

We call $\bar{t}_n$ the _dependencies_ of $p\{\bar{t}_n\}$, and $p$ _fresh_ for a set of terms $T$ if $p \notin \operatorname{par}(T)$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Proposition (Parameter Term).__
$\Gamma \vdash p\{\bar{t}_n\} : \bar{\upsilon}_m \!\to\! \varsigma$, and $p\{\bar{t}_n\}$ is in $\eta$-long $\beta$-normal form whenever $\bar{t}_n$ are.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

---

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Syntactic Equality).__ Two terms $s$ and $t$ are _syntactically equal_ if $s\theta = t\theta$ for all substitutions $\theta$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Pre-Unification Problem).__ Given a set of equations $\{\bar{s}_n \overset{?}{=} \bar{t}_n\}$, a _pre-unification problem_ is finding a _pre-unifier_ $\Theta = (\theta, C)$ such that $\bar{s}_n\theta$ and $\bar{t}_n\theta$ are syntactically equal modulo finitely many rewrite steps at any depth in terms on either side using the equational theory $C$.

## Classical Higher-Order Logic

Classical higher-order logic (HOL) is defined as an extension of STT and is the language we will be working with. For that, we consider the signature $\Sigma^{\mathrm{HOL}}$ defined as follows.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Signature of HOL).__ $\Sigma^{\textrm{HOL}}$ contains the following symbols:

$$
\top, \bot : o
\qquad\qquad
\neg : o \!\to\! o
\qquad\qquad
\land, \lor, \supset, \equiv \;: (o, o) \!\to\! o
$$
$$
\forall,\exists : [\alpha].\, (\alpha \!\to\! o) \!\to\! o
\qquad\qquad
=\; : [\alpha].\, (\alpha, \alpha) \!\to o
$$

We write $\varphi \oplus \psi$ instead of $\oplus(\varphi, \psi)$ for $\oplus \in \{\land, \lor, \supset, \equiv, =\}$ and $\mathrm{Q}\bar{\tau}_n.\, \varphi$ instead of $\mathrm{Q}(\lambda \tau_1.\, \dots \mathrm{Q}(\lambda \tau_n.\,\varphi))$ for $\mathrm{Q} \in \{\forall, \exists\}$. We call terms from $\Lambda(\Sigma^{\mathrm{HOL}})$ _HOL terms_ and to such of type $o$ as _formulae_ ($\varphi, \psi, \chi$).

```elixir
ShotDs.Hol.Definitions.signature()
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
["⊤", "⊥", "¬", "∨", "∧", "⊃", "≡", "=", "∀", "∃"]
```

__Definition (Frame).__ A _frame_ $\mathcal{D} = (\mathcal{D}_\tau)_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}}$ is a collection of nonempty sets $\mathcal{D}_\tau$ such that $\mathcal{D}_o = \{\mathrm{T}, \mathrm{F}\}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{\bar{\tau}_n\to\varsigma}$ is a set of functions mapping $\overline{\mathcal{D_{\tau_i}}}_n$ to $\mathcal{D}_\varsigma$.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Henkin Model).__ A _Henkin model_ is a tuple $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{D}, \mathcal{I})$, where $\mathcal{D}$ is a frame for which $\mathcal{D}_{\bar{\tau}\to\sigma}$ may contain only a subset of all functions from $\overline{\mathcal{D_{\tau_i}}}_n$ to $\mathcal{D}_\varsigma$ as long as every term still denotes, and $\mathcal{I}$ is a family of typed interpretation functions mapping $c\langle\bar{\tau}\rangle : \upsilon$ for $c \in \Sigma^{\mathrm{HOL}}$ and $\upsilon \in \mathcal{T}$ to appropriate elements in $\mathcal{D}_\upsilon$. $\mathcal{I}$ is defined as follows:

* $\mathcal{I}(\top) = \mathrm{T}$, $\mathcal{I}(\bot) = \mathrm{F}$
* $\mathcal{I}(\neg)$ maps $\mathrm{T}$ to $\mathrm{F}$ and $\mathrm{F}$ to $\mathrm{T}$
* $\mathcal{I}(\land)$, $\mathcal{I}(\lor)$, $\mathcal{I}(\supset)$ and $\mathcal{I}(\equiv)$ denote the standard Boolean functions for conjunction, disjunction, implication and equivalence
* $\mathcal{I}(=\!\langle o\rangle) = \mathcal{I}(\equiv)$
* $\mathcal{I}(=\!\langle \iota\rangle)$ denotes the Leibniz characterization of equality (two terms are equal if they share the same properties)
* $\mathcal{I}(=\!\langle(\tau_0, \bar{\tau}_n) \!\to\! \varsigma\rangle)$ denotes the function that maps $(s, t)$ to $\mathrm{T}$ iff $\mathcal{I}(=\!\langle(\bar{\tau}_n \!\to\! \varsigma\rangle)$ maps $(s~X, t~X)$ to $\mathrm{T}$ for all $X \in \mathcal{D}_{\tau_0}$
* $\mathcal{I}(\forall\langle\tau\rangle)$ maps property $t$ to $\mathrm{T}$ iff it holds for all elements in $\mathcal{D}_\tau$
* $\mathcal{I}(\exists\langle\tau\rangle)$ maps property $t$ to $\mathrm{T}$ iff it holds for some element in $\mathcal{D}_\tau$

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Denotation).__ Let $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{D}, \mathcal{I})$ be a Henkin model, $g$ be a variable assignemnt and $\bar{\xi}_k$ be a sequence of elements from $\mathcal{D}$. The _denotation_ of a HOL term $t : \tau$, denoted $[\![t]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi})$ is an element in $\mathcal{D}_\tau$ defined recursively:

* $[\![c]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k) = \mathcal{I}(c)$ for constant symbols $c \in \Sigma^{\mathrm{HOL}}$
* $[\![X]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k) = g(X)$ for variables $X \in \mathcal{V}$
* $[\![i]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k) = \xi_i$ for de Bruijn indices $i > 0$
* $[\![s(\bar{t}_n)]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k) = \left([\![s]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k)\right) \left(\overline{[\![t_i]\!](\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k)}_n \right)$
* $[\![\lambda\bar{\tau}_n.\, t : \bar{\upsilon}_m \!\to\! \varsigma]\!]^{\mathcal{M}, g, \bar{\xi}_k}$ is the function $f \in \mathcal{D}_{(\bar{\tau}_n, \bar{\upsilon}_m)\to\varsigma}$ such that for all $\bar{d}_n \in \overline{\mathcal{D}_{\tau_i}}_n$, $f(\bar{d}_n) = [\![t]\!]^{\mathcal{M}, g, (\overleftarrow{d}_n, \bar{\xi}_k)}$

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Satisfiability).__ A formula $\varphi$ is _satisfiable_ iff there exists a Henkin model $\mathcal{M}$ and a variable assignment $g$, such that $[\![\varphi]\!]^{\mathcal{M}, g, ()} = \mathrm{T}$. A set of formulae is satisfiable if all its elements are are jointly satisfiable under the same Henkin model and variable assignment. Conversely, a formula (or a set of formulae) is _unsatisfiable_ if no such model and variable assignment exists.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

__Definition (Entailment and Validity).__ A formula $\psi$ is _entailed_ by a set of formulae $\Phi$, denoted $\Phi \vdash \psi$ if $\Phi \cup \{\neg \psi\}$ is unsatisfiable. A formula $\varphi$ is _valid_, denoted $\vdash \varphi$, iff $\varnothing \vdash \varphi$.
